WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.095
Two busy threads share one CPU: the API that approves card payments, and the nightly settlement batch.

2
00:00:10.095 --> 00:00:11.183
Which one runs?

3
00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:19.267
Linux tracks each thread's virtual runtime, the CPU time it has had so far, and runs whichever has had the least.

4
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:25.425
The API runs a 3 millisecond slice, so now the batch is behind, and it goes next.

5
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:31.904
They take turns and split it evenly: in the lab, 4.99 seconds each, out of 10.

6
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.337
But the batch matters less, so give it nice 10.

7
00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:39.815
Nice runs from minus 20 to 19, and higher is nicer to the others.

8
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:46.141
Each nice value has a weight: 1024 for nice 0, 110 for nice 10.

9
00:00:46.141 --> 00:00:52.249
Virtual runtime grows by real time, times 1024 over the weight.

10
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:59.224
So the batch's clock runs 9.3 times faster: one 3 millisecond slice adds 28.

11
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:06.053
The API is now far behind, so it runs nine slices in a row before the batch is lowest again.

12
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.892
That's 90 percent against 10: less CPU for the batch, never none.

13
00:01:11.892 --> 00:01:15.935
The lab box measured 9.01 seconds against 0.98.

14
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:19.387
A container needs a ceiling instead.

15
00:01:19.387 --> 00:01:26.707
Half a CPU becomes a budget in cpu.max: 50 milliseconds of CPU time in every 100.

16
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:28.525
The budget is CPU time.

17
00:01:28.525 --> 00:01:34.005
Four busy threads, on four CPUs, spend it all in 12 and a half milliseconds.

18
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:41.379
Then the kernel stops every thread in the container until the next period: about 87 milliseconds of nothing.

19
00:01:41.379 --> 00:01:42.576
That's throttling.

20
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:45.689
A request arriving in the gap just waits.

21
00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:51.889
In the lab, 40 of 41 periods were throttled, and the longest stall was 94 milliseconds.

22
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:59.680
The thread with the least weighted runtime runs next, and a container that spends its quota early waits for the next period.
